    text: "Solon's reforms of 594 BCE cancelled debts and forbade enslaving Athenians for debt, the seisachtheia or shaking-off of burdens. Borrowing went on. A citizen in trouble first raised an eranos, an interest-free loan collected from friends, repaid in instalments and a matter of honour. Land mortgaged against a loan was marked by a horos stone recording the debt, and many survive. Bankers, trapezitai, sat at tables in the Agora and lent at interest; the most famous, Pasion, began as a slave and died a rich citizen. Collecting a debt meant going to court, and lawsuits over unpaid loans fill the speeches of Demosthenes.",
