    text: "Roman farming ranged from a peasant's few iugera of wheat, beans and vines to the latifundia, great estates worked by slave gangs and later by tenant coloni. Cato, Varro and Columella wrote manuals for owners on when to plough, how to prune vines, and how much to feed a slave. Wheat and barley were sown in autumn and reaped with sickles in early summer. Livestock was big business: flocks of sheep walked the calles, public drove roads, between summer pastures in the Apennines and winter grazing on the plains of Apulia, and the state charged a fee per head. Shepherds lived rough with their dogs, and landowners feared them as half-bandits.",
