    text: "A Viking-age farm in Scandinavia had its infield, the fenced, manured land nearest the house where barley, oats and rye grew and hay was cut, and beyond it the outfield of rough grazing, woods and moor. The ard and the sickle were the main tools; iron ploughshares came later. Cattle, sheep, goats, pigs and horses were the farm's wealth, stalled through the long winter in a byre, often under the same roof as the family, and fed on hay and leaf fodder. In summer the herds were driven to upland pastures. The work was shared by a household of family, servants and slaves, thralls, who did the dirtiest jobs.",
