    text: "Hunting mattered more in the north than in the Frankish lands. In the forests of Sweden and Norway people dug trapping pits along elk and reindeer paths, lines of them still visible today, and trapped marten, squirrel, beaver and fox for furs, which the Norse traded south to Frankish and Arab buyers. From the Sámi they took furs, down and walrus hide as tribute, as the Norwegian Ohthere told King Alfred. On lakes and coasts, fishing with nets, hooks and weirs fed households year round, and seals and seabirds were hunted on the skerries. Walrus ivory from the far north paid for much of the Norse settlement of Greenland.",
