    text: "Scandinavia was rich in bog iron, ore that forms in wetlands and could be dug and smelted in small clay furnaces with charcoal, producing blooms that smiths hammered into tools, rivets and weapons. In Norway people quarried soapstone, carving pots and loom weights straight from the rock face, where the scars of removed bowls can still be seen. In towns such as Birka and Hedeby specialist craftsmen worked antler into combs, cast bronze brooches in clay moulds, made glass beads and worked amber. Most crafts, weaving, woodwork, boat-repair, were done on the farm, and a good carpenter or shipwright was valued above many warriors.",
