    text: "The Norse moved goods by water whenever they could. Cargo ships such as the knarr carried furs, iron, walrus ivory, dried fish and slaves across the North Sea and Baltic, and river boats worked the Russian waterways to Constantinople and the Caspian, hauled overland at portages. At home, winter was the travelling season: once the lakes and bogs froze, sledges pulled by horses and people on skis and skates moved easily over routes impassable in summer. Markets were held at seasonal meeting places and in the new towns, Birka on Lake Mälaren, Hedeby, Kaupang and Ribe, where silver was weighed on folding scales and merchants paid the king for protection.",
