    text: "The Norse ate barley porridge and flatbread, dairy of every kind, butter, cheese and skyr, a cultured milk, meat, especially in autumn when surplus animals were slaughtered, and fish fresh, dried, salted or fermented. Food for the winter was the household's first concern: meat and fish were smoked, dried and salted, butter packed in casks, and whey used to pickle; much was kept in a separate storehouse, the stabbur of later Norway, raised on posts against damp and vermin. A housewife counted what was left in late winter against the weeks until spring. Water came from wells lined with timber and stones and from streams, and ale was brewed for feasts.",
