    text: "Across much of medieval Europe a village's arable lay in great open fields divided into strips, with each household's strips scattered among its neighbours'; the village decided together when to plough, sow and let the animals onto the stubble. Beyond lay the commons, meadow for hay and waste for grazing, governed by village by-laws, and the woods where pigs were fattened. The wild was increasingly fenced in by lords: royal forests with their own harsh law, private chases, warrens where rabbits were bred for their fur and meat, and fishponds, stews, kept stocked with carp and bream for fast days. Poaching deer or fish from these was a crime, and commonly done.",
