    text: "Around 1268 Étienne Boileau, provost of Paris, recorded the statutes of about a hundred crafts in the Livre des métiers: who could be a master, how many apprentices each could take, working hours, and which materials were forbidden. Florence had its arti, from the great Calimala and Lana guilds to the lesser trades. Crafts clustered by street, and a workshop was also a shop and a home. Each guild or its confraternity had a patron saint, Eloi for smiths, Crispin for shoemakers, Luke for painters, an altar, a candle kept burning, a feast day, and a fund for burying members. Stone for the cathedrals was quarried from limestone galleries beneath and around Paris, the origin of its later catacombs.",
