    title: "Les Halles, the water merchants and the commune",
    text: "In 1183 Philip Augustus built covered markets at Les Halles, which became the belly of Paris for seven centuries. Goods came up the Seine, and the river trade was controlled by the Hanse of water merchants, whose leader, the prévôt des marchands, became head of the city's government; the ship on Paris's arms comes from them. Porters, carters and boatmen formed their own trades. Across Europe towns won charters and communes, electing consuls or aldermen, keeping a seal, a bell and a town hall. In Florence the guildsmen themselves ran the republic: the priors of the Signoria had to be members of a guild, and a nobleman who wanted office had to join one.",
