    title: "Pottage, bread and the Lenten fast",
    text: "Medieval households lived on bread and pottage. Peasants ate coarse bread of rye, barley or maslin, a wheat-rye mix, and a stew of peas, beans, onions, leeks and cabbage kept on the fire, with bacon when they had it; ale in the north, wine in the south. In towns, most bought bread from bakers, whose loaves were checked for weight under assizes of bread. The Church's calendar shaped every menu: on Fridays, in Advent and through the forty days of Lent, meat, and often eggs and dairy, were forbidden, so salt herring, stockfish and almond milk filled in. Pigs were killed in November and salted or smoked, and the stores of grain, peas and bacon had to last until harvest.",
