    text: "A medieval townhouse stood on a burgage plot, a long, narrow strip with a shopfront on the street and a yard, workshop and garden behind, held for a money rent that freed the burgess from rural services. Houses were timber-framed with wattle-and-daub or brick infill, their upper storeys jutting over the street, and thatch was increasingly banned for fear of fire. Between 1100 and 1350 lords founded hundreds of new towns, bastides in southern France and villeneuves elsewhere, on a planned grid, offering plots and freedoms to attract settlers; in the east, German settlers moved into Bohemia, Poland and the Baltic lands in the Ostsiedlung under German town law.",
