    text: "Around 1393 an elderly, prosperous Parisian wrote a book of instruction for his fifteen-year-old bride, Le Ménagier de Paris. It covers prayer and obedience, but mostly the practical partnership of a household: how to hire and manage servants, keep the garden, get fleas out of a bedroom, buy meat at the market and cook a feast. He expected her to remarry after his death and wanted her to do him credit. In artisan families especially, marriage was a working partnership: wives kept shop, dealt with customers and kept accounts, and guild rules often let a master's widow carry on his workshop. Husband and wife built a household's future together, or not at all.",
