    text: "Medieval people went to law readily, before manor courts, town courts, royal courts and the church courts, which handled marriage, wills, defamation and oaths. Yet much conflict was settled out of court. Neighbours chose arbitrators, often the parish priest or respected men of the village, and bound themselves to accept the award under penalty. In Italian towns, notaries drew up formal instrumenta pacis, peace contracts, ending vendettas between families, sealed with a kiss of peace. Insults, especially to a woman's sexual honour or a man's honesty, were taken seriously enough to be sued for as defamation, and public apology in church before the parish was a common penalty.",
