    text: "The Novgorod countryside was poor for grain: short summers, sandy and boggy soils, and forest to be cleared by burning, the podseka, before rye, barley and oats could be sown. Bad harvests meant buying grain from the south. Cows, horses and pigs grazed woodland clearings and meadows along the rivers, and hay had to be cut for seven months of winter. The real wealth was in the forest and waters. Trappers took squirrel, marten and sable, and Novgorod collected furs as tribute from Finnic peoples across the north to the Urals. Beekeepers harvested wild honey and wax from hollow trees, bortnichestvo, and fishermen worked Lake Ilmen and the Volkhov with nets and weirs.",
