    text: "The food of the Rus north was rye bread, baked in the great clay stove, kasha of buckwheat, millet or oats, shchi, a cabbage soup, turnips, peas and mushrooms, with fish more often than meat because the Orthodox calendar had some two hundred fast days a year. Kvass, a mildly fermented drink from rye bread, and mead were the everyday drinks. Stores were kept in a cellar under the house or a separate storehouse: barrels of salted cabbage, pickled mushrooms, salt fish, honey, and grain in bins, checked anxiously through the long winter. Women did the cooking, baking and brewing. Water came from the river or from wells lined with timber, many found in Novgorod's excavations.",
