    text: "Around 1260 a Novgorod boy named Onfim, aged about six or seven, practised his letters on birch bark: the alphabet, syllables, bits of psalms, and, in the margins, drawings of himself as a mounted warrior spearing an enemy. His exercises survive because Novgorod's wet soil kept them. They show that townsfolk taught children to read, probably from a priest or deacon, for business as much as prayer; birch-bark letters were written by merchants, artisans and women alike. Other children learned their parents' craft or went to a master. The old were cared for by their children under Orthodox teaching of honouring parents, and many elderly took monastic vows to end their days in a monastery.",
