    text: "Birch-bark letter no. 377 from Novgorod, of the late thirteenth century, reads as a proposal: Mikita writes to Ulyanitsa asking her to marry him, 'I want you, and you me'. Most matches were arranged by families, often through a svakha, a matchmaker, and settled with a dowry of household goods, clothes and sometimes land; the church required consent and forbade marriage within close degrees. Weddings joined church blessing with long folk rituals of lament by the bride, bread, and the unbraiding of her single plait into the two of a married woman. Husband and wife worked the household together, and letters show wives running business while husbands travelled.",
