    text: "A large share of Novgorod's birch-bark letters are about money owed: lists of debtors, demands to pay up, instructions to collect a debt in the countryside. The Russkaya Pravda regulated lending, capping interest in some cases and setting rules for collection. A poor man who took a loan could become a zakup, a half-free dependant who worked for his creditor until he repaid, and if he ran away he became a slave, a kholop. In famine years, such as the terrible hunger of 1230, chroniclers record people selling themselves and their children for bread.",
