    text: "Byzantine villages were tax communities, the chorion, jointly responsible for their taxes. The Farmer's Law, probably eighth century, deals with farmers' daily troubles: oxen that stray into a neighbour's vines, a herdsman who loses a cow, trees planted on another's land. A family with a pair of oxen, the zeugaratos, was the fiscal norm. They grew wheat and barley, vines and olives; flocks of sheep and goats moved between lowland and mountain grazing, and Vlach shepherds became known for transhumance. At Constantinople the Bosphorus was one of the richest fisheries in the world: bonito, mackerel and tunny passed through it in autumn, and fishermen worked from fixed stations, the epochai, with nets and lamps.",
