    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Scrubbed stoops, hutspot and the stores",
    text: "Foreign visitors marvelled at Dutch housewives' cleanliness: stoops and pavements scrubbed daily, floors sanded, pots scoured. The housewife ran the home with a maid or two if she could, keeping accounts, buying at market and preserving. A Dutch household ate rye and wheat bread, butter and cheese, porridge, beans and peas, root vegetables stewed into hutspot, salt herring and beer; the wealthy added meat, sugar and spices from the East Indies. English households ate bread, cheese and pottage, and beef when they could. Stores were laid down in autumn, salted meat, smoked fish, barrels of beer, apples and roots, and cookbooks such as De verstandige kok gave recipes for keeping them.",
    wiki: ["Hutspot", "Dutch cuisine", "Dutch Golden Age"],
