    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Brick gables, rented rooms and newcomers",
    text: "Fire rules after the great blazes of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries pushed north-western cities to brick and tile. Amsterdam's canal houses were tall, narrow and deep, taxed by frontage, with hooks on the gable to hoist goods and furniture; most townsfolk, though, rented rooms, cellars and back houses, and moved often. London rebuilt in brick after the fire of 1666. The Dutch Republic grew on migrants: Flemish Protestants fleeing Spain, Sephardic Jews from Portugal, Huguenots after 1685, and tens of thousands of Germans and Scandinavians who came as sailors, soldiers and servants. Many newcomers arrived with nothing and built a household in a single room.",
    wiki: ["Canals of Amsterdam", "Huguenots", "Great Fire of London"],
