    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Journeyman's papers, the Meister and the Ruhr pits",
    text: "In Germany and Austria the craft trades kept a formal ladder: an apprentice, Lehrling, trained three years in a master's workshop and at trade school, passed the journeyman's examination, and after years of work could take the master craftsman's examination; from 1935 only a Meister could train apprentices or open a shop. Some journeymen still went on the Wanderschaft in black corduroy. Big industry, Siemens in Berlin, Krupp in Essen, ran its own apprentice schools. Miners in the Ruhr and Silesia hewed coal with pneumatic picks, and kept their old greeting Glück auf and their patron Saint Barbara, whose day, 4 December, was still marked underground.",
    wiki: ["Meister", "Wandering journeyman", "Saint Barbara"],
