    title: "Empty pews, the Confessing Church and the saints' days",
    text: "Interwar Berlin was one of Europe's most secular cities: hundreds of thousands formally left the Protestant church, and socialist freethinkers held Jugendweihe ceremonies in place of confirmation. Catholic regions such as Bavaria, the Rhineland and Poland kept patron-saint festivals, processions and pilgrimages to shrines such as Altötting and Częstochowa. Under Nazism the Protestant church split between the regime-friendly German Christians and the Confessing Church, and Catholic clergy were harassed; in the Soviet Union churches were closed and priests shot. War brought people back to prayer, candles for the missing, and memorial masses.",
