    title: "Modernist estates, refugees and the expellees",
    text: "Housing reform after 1918 built new estates for working families: in Berlin, the Horseshoe Estate at Britz, with its gardens and light, bright flats, was designed by Bruno Taut in the 1920s. For millions, though, the move to a new home was forced. The war and its end uprooted Europe: some twelve million Germans fled or were expelled from Silesia, East Prussia, the Sudetenland and elsewhere in 1944–50, and were billeted on unwilling families in the west; Poles moved from the eastern borderlands into former German towns. Displaced persons camps held survivors and forced labourers. Making a new home meant starting from a single rented room.",
