    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The health-insurance dentist and the drill",
    text: "By the interwar years most German workers belonged to a statutory health insurance fund, a Krankenkasse, which paid for basic dental care: extractions, amalgam fillings and dentures. Dentists used electric drills and local anaesthetic injections of procaine, marketed as Novocaine, so a filling was no longer torture. Rural people and the poor elsewhere in Europe still pulled bad teeth rather than fill them. School dental clinics spread in German and British cities. War brought shortages of anaesthetic, gold and dentists, and in the camps and ghettos there was no care at all; the Nazi state even stripped gold fillings from murdered prisoners.",
